window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"DevOps Field Guide","section":"Become a DevOps engineer.","tool":"home","text":"Become a DevOps engineer. A sharp, visual, interview-ready path from the basics to pro — CI/CD, containers, Kubernetes, Terraform, AWS, deployment strategies, and reliability . Every idea gets a plain-English analogy, a diagram, and the trade-off an interviewer will probe. AWS-first, as of June 2026","keywords":["become","devops","engineer"]},{"url":"index.html#progress","page":"DevOps Field Guide","section":"Your progress","tool":"home","text":"01 Your progress Mark lessons \"learned\" as you go — your progress is saved in this browser. Pick up where you left off, or jump to a module below. Start learning Reset progress","keywords":["your","progress"]},{"url":"index.html#path","page":"DevOps Field Guide","section":"The journey, junior to pro","tool":"home","text":"02 The journey, junior to pro DevOps is a layered skill: get fluent with the OS and pipelines, own the platform with containers and infra, then run it in production with safe deploys and reliability. Junior · get fluent The fundamentals What DevOps is, the Linux + networking bedrock, and Git/CI so e","keywords":["the","journey","junior","to","pro"]},{"url":"index.html#modules","page":"DevOps Field Guide","section":"Explore the modules","tool":"home","text":"03 Explore the modules Nine modules plus a dedicated interview hub. Start at the top or dive into whatever you're prepping. Foundations What & why DevOps culture, the delivery lifecycle, and the DORA metrics that define \"good.\" Start -> Bedrock Linux & networking Processes, signals, permissions; DNS","keywords":["explore","the","modules"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-devops.html","page":"What DevOps really is","section":"What is DevOps?","tool":"foundations","text":"What is DevOps? DevOps is a culture and a set of practices that shorten and de-risk the loop from writing code to running it in production. It is not a job title you hire or a tool you buy.","keywords":["what","is","devops"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-devops.html#idea","page":"What DevOps really is","section":"A culture, not a product","tool":"foundations","text":"01 A culture, not a product DevOps = shared ownership of the whole delivery loop, with automation and fast feedback so changes ship small, often, and safely. It tears down the old split where developers wrote code and a separate ops team ran it. The aim is one team accountable from idea to productio","keywords":["culture","not","product"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-devops.html#wall","page":"What DevOps really is","section":"The wall of confusion","tool":"foundations","text":"02 The wall of confusion Before DevOps, devs were rewarded for change (ship features) and ops for stability (don't break prod). Those incentives fight. Code got \"thrown over the wall,\" and when it broke, each side blamed the other. Wall of confusion Dev throws a release over to ops, then moves on. O","keywords":["the","wall","of","confusion"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-devops.html#calms","page":"What DevOps really is","section":"CALMS — the five pillars","tool":"foundations","text":"03 CALMS — the five pillars CALMS is the standard checklist for \"are we actually doing DevOps?\" It is culture-first: tools alone change nothing if the incentives and ownership don't move. Culture Shared goals and blameless learning. Dev and ops win or lose together, not against each other. Automatio","keywords":["calms","the","five","pillars"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-devops.html#vs-sre-platform","page":"What DevOps really is","section":"DevOps vs SRE vs Platform Engineering","tool":"foundations","text":"04 DevOps vs SRE vs Platform Engineering These overlap and interviewers love the distinction. DevOps is the philosophy ; SRE is one concrete implementation of it; Platform Engineering is the team-and-product approach that makes the philosophy easy to follow at scale. What it is Key idea DevOps A cul","keywords":["devops","vs","sre","platform","engineering"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-devops.html#interview","page":"What DevOps really is","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What is DevOps? warm-up Model answer DevOps is a culture and set of practices that shorten and de-risk the loop from writing code to running it — through shared ownership, automation, and fast feedback. It's summarized by CALMS (Culture, Automation, Lean, Measurement, Sharing","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/lifecycle-and-toolchain.html","page":"The delivery lifecycle & 2026 toolchain","section":"The delivery lifecycle & toolchain.","tool":"foundations","text":"The delivery lifecycle & toolchain. A change flows down one conveyor — plan → code → build → test → release → deploy → operate — and the build produces one artifact that is promoted, unchanged, all the way to production.","keywords":["the","delivery","lifecycle","toolchain"]},{"url":"foundations/lifecycle-and-toolchain.html#lifecycle","page":"The delivery lifecycle & 2026 toolchain","section":"One conveyor, seven stages","tool":"foundations","text":"01 One conveyor, seven stages The lifecycle is a pipeline : each stage adds something and hands a standard part to the next. The golden rule — build once, promote the same artifact everywhere. You don't recompile per environment. CI builds a versioned image; staging and prod run those exact bytes. T","keywords":["one","conveyor","seven","stages"]},{"url":"foundations/lifecycle-and-toolchain.html#stages","page":"The delivery lifecycle & 2026 toolchain","section":"What each stage does","tool":"foundations","text":"02 What each stage does Two halves: the left side (plan→test) is mostly developer flow and CI; the right side (release→operate) is delivery and running the service. The handoff between them is the artifact . Plan Decide what to build — issues, tickets, design. The backlog feeds the conveyor. Code Wr","keywords":["what","each","stage","does","main"]},{"url":"foundations/lifecycle-and-toolchain.html#toolchain","page":"The delivery lifecycle & 2026 toolchain","section":"The 2026 toolchain map","tool":"foundations","text":"03 The 2026 toolchain map Don't memorize every product — memorize the categories and one or two AWS-first defaults per slot. Tools change; the shape of the pipeline doesn't. Category What it does 2026 defaults (AWS-first) SCM Source of truth for code + history. Git on GitHub (also GitLab) CI Build +","keywords":["the","toolchain","map"]},{"url":"foundations/lifecycle-and-toolchain.html#artifact","page":"The delivery lifecycle & 2026 toolchain","section":"Why \"build once\" matters","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Why \"build once\" matters An artifact is the single immutable output of the build — usually a versioned container image, tagged by commit SHA. It's the contract between CI and CD: CI produces it, CD only ever promotes it. promote.sh Copy # Built ONCE in CI, tagged by commit: IMG=123.dkr.ecr.us-eas","keywords":["why","build","once","matters","Built","ONCE","in","CI","tagged","by","commit","IMG","123","dkr","ecr","us","east","amazonaws","com","api","git","af2c1","Staging","and","prod","deploy","the","SAME","digest","no","rebuild","kubectl","set","image","staging","identical","bytes","docker"]},{"url":"foundations/lifecycle-and-toolchain.html#interview","page":"The delivery lifecycle & 2026 toolchain","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Walk me through your delivery pipeline. warm-up Model answer A push to Git fires a webhook to CI (GitHub Actions). CI checks out the commit, runs lint + tests, and on green builds one versioned image and pushes it to ECR. CD (Argo CD) then promotes that exact image through st","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/dora-and-delivery-metrics.html","page":"DORA & delivery metrics","section":"DORA & delivery metrics.","tool":"foundations","text":"DORA & delivery metrics. The DORA four keys turn \"are we good at shipping?\" into numbers. Crucially, the research found elite teams are both fast and stable — speed and safety aren't a trade-off when the system is well-built.","keywords":["dora","delivery","metrics"]},{"url":"foundations/dora-and-delivery-metrics.html#four-keys","page":"DORA & delivery metrics","section":"The four keys","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The four keys DORA measures delivery on throughput (how fast) and stability (how safe). Two metrics each. From years of research by the DevOps Research and Assessment group, these four predict software delivery performance. They describe the system , not any individual engineer. In plain English ","keywords":["the","four","keys"]},{"url":"foundations/dora-and-delivery-metrics.html#not-a-tradeoff","page":"DORA & delivery metrics","section":"Speed vs stability is a false choice","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Speed vs stability is a false choice The headline finding: throughput and stability move together . Teams that deploy more often also break prod less and recover faster — because small, frequent, automated changes are easy to test, review, and reverse. Pro tip tip \"Slow and careful\" is the trap i","keywords":["speed","vs","stability","is","false","choice"]},{"url":"foundations/dora-and-delivery-metrics.html#tiers","page":"DORA & delivery metrics","section":"Performance tiers","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Performance tiers DORA buckets teams into Elite / High / Medium / Low across the four keys. You don't need exact thresholds in an interview — know the shape : elite is on-demand deploys, sub-hour lead time, low failure rate, and recovery in minutes. Metric Elite Low Deployment frequency On-demand","keywords":["performance","tiers"]},{"url":"foundations/dora-and-delivery-metrics.html#improve-and-traps","page":"DORA & delivery metrics","section":"Improving them — and gaming them","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Improving them — and gaming them The four keys are connected, so the same fixes lift several at once. Shrink the batch, automate the path, and add safe rollback — throughput and stability climb together. 1 Smaller batches Trunk-based dev, small PRs behind feature flags. Cuts lead time and makes f","keywords":["improving","them","and","gaming"]},{"url":"foundations/dora-and-delivery-metrics.html#interview","page":"DORA & delivery metrics","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Name the four DORA metrics. warm-up Model answer Deployment frequency and lead time for changes are the throughput pair; change failure rate and failed-deployment recovery time (MTTR) are the stability pair. Together they capture how fast and how safely a team delivers softwa","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"linux-networking/linux-for-devops.html","page":"Linux for DevOps","section":"Linux for DevOps.","tool":"linux-networking","text":"Linux for DevOps. Every container, every EC2 box, every CI runner is a Linux machine. You don't need to be a kernel hacker — but processes, signals, permissions, and systemd are the wiring behind everything you deploy.","keywords":["linux","for","devops"]},{"url":"linux-networking/linux-for-devops.html#idea","page":"Linux for DevOps","section":"The mental model","tool":"linux-networking","text":"01 The mental model A running program is a process with a PID . The kernel talks to it with signals , the filesystem gates it with permissions , and systemd keeps long-lived ones alive. Master these four and Linux stops being a black box. Almost every DevOps incident bottoms out here: a process that","keywords":["the","mental","model","systemd","SIGTERM","SIGKILL"]},{"url":"linux-networking/linux-for-devops.html#signals","page":"Linux for DevOps","section":"Signals & graceful shutdown","tool":"linux-networking","text":"02 Signals & graceful shutdown SIGTERM is a polite \"please stop and clean up.\" SIGKILL ( kill -9 ) is forced — the kernel reaps the process instantly, no chance to react. This single distinction governs whether your deploys lose in-flight requests. When an orchestrator rolls a deployment it sends SI","keywords":["signals","graceful","shutdown","SIGTERM","SIGKILL","kill","ps","aux","grep","gunicorn","find","the","PID","default","polite","stop","TERM","same","thing","explicit","forced","NO","cleanup","trap","echo","draining","exit","an","app","trapping","CMD","bin","sh"]},{"url":"linux-networking/linux-for-devops.html#perms-systemd","page":"Linux for DevOps","section":"Permissions, users & systemd","tool":"linux-networking","text":"03 Permissions, users & systemd Files carry rwx bits for owner / group / others, plus an owning user. The rule for production: run as a non-root user with only the access it needs. systemd is how Linux starts, supervises, and restarts your daemons. Read -rwxr-x--- left to right: a regular file the o","keywords":["permissions","users","systemd","rwxr","chmod","chown","ps","aux","top","htop","kill","pgrep","nice","ls","stat","find","df","du","sh","lsblk","mount","ss","tulpn","curl","dig","ip","systemctl","status","start","restart","journalctl","journald","app","Service","ExecStart","usr","bin","python","main","py"]},{"url":"linux-networking/linux-for-devops.html#scripts","page":"Linux for DevOps","section":"Robust shell & \"a container is just a process\"","tool":"linux-networking","text":"04 Robust shell & \"a container is just a process\" Every command returns an exit code in $? — 0 means success, non-zero means failure. CI and shells branch on it. A safe script starts with set -euo pipefail so failures stop the script instead of silently rolling on. Pipelines ( | ) stream one command","keywords":["robust","shell","container","is","just","process","set","euo","pipefail","usr","bin","env","bash","exit","on","error","undefined","var","failed","pipe","stage","fails","the","line","IMAGE_TAG","must","be","require","an","or","bail","docker","build","app","echo","code","was","success","SIGTERM","SIGKILL"]},{"url":"linux-networking/linux-for-devops.html#interview","page":"Linux for DevOps","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"linux-networking","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire SIGTERM vs SIGKILL — and why does it matter for containers? core Model answer SIGTERM is catchable: the app can trap it, stop accepting new requests, drain in-flight ones, flush state, and exit cleanly. SIGKILL ( kill -9 ) can't be caught — the kernel kills the process instan","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","SIGTERM","SIGKILL","kill","bin","sh","terminationGracePeriodSeconds","USER","set","euo","pipefail"]},{"url":"linux-networking/networking-for-devops.html","page":"Networking for DevOps","section":"Networking for DevOps.","tool":"linux-networking","text":"Networking for DevOps. Half of debugging cloud infra is networking: a DNS record that didn't propagate, a security group that drops traffic, a subnet with no route out. Learn how a request really travels from a browser to your pod.","keywords":["networking","for","devops"]},{"url":"linux-networking/networking-for-devops.html#idea","page":"Networking for DevOps","section":"The request's journey","tool":"linux-networking","text":"01 The request's journey A request crosses a chain: DNS turns a name into an IP, TCP/TLS opens a secure pipe, a load balancer picks a healthy target, and a security group decides whether the packet is even allowed in. Break any link and the page won't load. You rarely configure one box anymore — you","keywords":["the","request","journey"]},{"url":"linux-networking/networking-for-devops.html#dns-transport","page":"Networking for DevOps","section":"DNS, ports & TCP vs UDP","tool":"linux-networking","text":"02 DNS, ports & TCP vs UDP DNS maps names to addresses; an A record points a name at an IP, a CNAME points a name at another name. Records are cached for their TTL — which is why a DNS change isn't instant. Transport is either TCP (reliable, ordered) or UDP (fast, fire-and-forget). Dimension TCP UDP","keywords":["dns","ports","tcp","vs","udp","dig","shop","example","com","short","the","ALIAS","record","an","IP","CNAME","is","it","alias","to","another","name","curl","https","dev","null","watch","TCP","TLS","handshake"]},{"url":"linux-networking/networking-for-devops.html#vpc-cidr","page":"Networking for DevOps","section":"CIDR, subnets, NAT & load balancers","tool":"linux-networking","text":"03 CIDR, subnets, NAT & load balancers A CIDR block like 10.0.0.0/16 defines an IP range; the number after / is how many bits are fixed as the network prefix. A VPC is split into public subnets (route to the internet) and private subnets (no inbound from the internet — outbound via NAT ). Read a CID","keywords":["cidr","subnets","nat","load","balancers","api","img"]},{"url":"linux-networking/networking-for-devops.html#firewalls","page":"Networking for DevOps","section":"Firewalls: security groups vs NACLs","tool":"linux-networking","text":"04 Firewalls: security groups vs NACLs AWS gives you two firewalls. Security groups wrap a resource (an instance/ENI) and are stateful — if you allow a request out or in, the return traffic is automatically allowed. NACLs wrap a subnet and are stateless — you must allow both directions explicitly. S","keywords":["firewalls","security","groups","vs","nacls"]},{"url":"linux-networking/networking-for-devops.html#interview","page":"Networking for DevOps","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"linux-networking","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between an L4 and an L7 load balancer? core Model answer An L4 load balancer (AWS NLB) works at the transport layer — it forwards TCP/UDP based only on IP and port, doesn't understand HTTP, and is built for raw throughput and low latency. An L7 load bala","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","255"]},{"url":"cicd/git-and-branching.html","page":"Git & branching models","section":"Git & branching.","tool":"cicd","text":"Git & branching. Git is the single source of truth for your code — and increasingly your infra too. How you branch decides how fast you can ship: short-lived branches feed CI, long-lived ones starve it. This is the workflow interviewers probe first.","keywords":["git","branching"]},{"url":"cicd/git-and-branching.html#idea","page":"Git & branching models","section":"Git as the source of truth","tool":"cicd","text":"01 Git as the source of truth Git records every change as an immutable commit on a branch; main is the authoritative state of the product. The branching model you pick is a contract for how work merges back — and it directly enables or sabotages continuous integration. Two models dominate interviews","keywords":["git","as","the","source","of","truth","main","develop","release","feature","hotfix"]},{"url":"cicd/git-and-branching.html#models","page":"Git & branching models","section":"Trunk-based vs GitFlow","tool":"cicd","text":"02 Trunk-based vs GitFlow Trunk-based : branch off main , do a day's work, open a small PR, merge — unfinished features hide behind feature flags . GitFlow : parallel long-lived branches with a heavier merge ritual, suited to versioned or desktop releases. GitFlow — heavyweight Long-lived develop , ","keywords":["trunk","based","vs","gitflow","main","develop","release","feature","hotfix"]},{"url":"cicd/git-and-branching.html#merge-rebase","page":"Git & branching models","section":"Merge vs rebase","tool":"cicd","text":"03 Merge vs rebase Merge joins two branches with a merge commit — true history, but a tangled graph. Rebase replays your commits on top of the latest main — a clean, linear history. The iron rule: never rebase a shared/pushed branch , because it rewrites commits others already have. Merge Rebase His","keywords":["merge","vs","rebase","main","Safe","tidy","your","own","un","pushed","feature","branch","onto","latest","git","fetch","origin","replay","my","commits","on","top","of","DANGEROUS","never","do","this","to","teammates","have","pulled","shared","rewrites","history","breaks","others"]},{"url":"cicd/git-and-branching.html#pr-release","page":"Git & branching models","section":"PR workflow, tags & repo shape","tool":"cicd","text":"04 PR workflow, tags & repo shape Changes reach main through a pull request gated by required reviews and status checks (CI must be green). Releases are cut by tagging a commit with a semantic version . And you'll be asked: monorepo or polyrepo? 1 Open a PR Small diff off a short-lived branch. Branc","keywords":["pr","workflow","tags","repo","shape","main","v2","MAJOR","MINOR","PATCH","git","checkout","pull","tag","release","annotated","on","this","commit","push","origin","pushing","the","triggers","pipeline","feature","login","rebase"]},{"url":"cicd/git-and-branching.html#interview","page":"Git & branching models","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"cicd","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Trunk-based vs GitFlow — which do you use and why? core Model answer For a service that ships continuously, trunk-based: short-lived branches merged to main at least daily, with unfinished work behind feature flags. It keeps PRs small, conflicts tiny, and — crucially — actual","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","main","develop","release","feature","hotfix","MAJOR","MINOR","PATCH"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-integration.html","page":"Continuous integration","section":"Continuous integration.","tool":"cicd","text":"Continuous integration. Every commit is built and tested automatically against shared main , so breakage surfaces in minutes — not on release day. CI is the heartbeat of a delivery pipeline.","keywords":["continuous","integration"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-integration.html#idea","page":"Continuous integration","section":"What CI actually is","tool":"cicd","text":"01 What CI actually is CI = merge small changes often , and have a robot build + test every change before it reaches the trunk. The goal is a main branch that is always releasable . A push (or pull request) fires a webhook; a fresh runner checks out the code, installs dependencies, runs lint + tests","keywords":["what","ci","actually","is","git"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-integration.html#pipeline","page":"Continuous integration","section":"Anatomy of a pipeline","tool":"cicd","text":"02 Anatomy of a pipeline A CI job is just ordered steps in a YAML file the platform runs for you. Here's a minimal GitHub Actions workflow — the shape is the same on GitLab CI or Jenkins. .github/workflows/ci.yml Copy on: pull_request jobs: test: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkou","keywords":["anatomy","of","pipeline","on","pull_request","jobs","test","runs","ubuntu","latest","steps","uses","actions","checkout","v4","setup","python","v5","with","version","cache","pip","run","install","requirements","txt","ruff","check","lint","pytest","tests","the","gate"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-integration.html#green","page":"Continuous integration","section":"Keep main green","tool":"cicd","text":"03 Keep main green CI only pays off if changes are small and frequent . Long-lived branches defeat the point: you integrate rarely, so conflicts and surprises pile up — the exact pain CI exists to kill. Defeats CI Feature branch lives for 3 weeks. One giant PR, hundreds of files. Tests run only befo","keywords":["keep","main","green"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-integration.html#interview","page":"Continuous integration","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"cicd","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between continuous integration, delivery, and deployment? warm-up Model answer CI = every change is automatically built and tested against main. Continuous delivery = every green build is automatically packaged and ready to release, but a human clicks \"g","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-delivery.html","page":"Continuous delivery & deployment","section":"Continuous delivery.","tool":"cicd","text":"Continuous delivery. CI ends with a tested artifact. Continuous delivery is everything after: that one artifact is promoted through environments — same bytes, different config — until it's sitting on the loading dock, ready to ship the instant a human says \"go.\"","keywords":["continuous","delivery"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-delivery.html#delivery-vs-deployment","page":"Continuous delivery & deployment","section":"Delivery vs deployment","tool":"cicd","text":"01 Delivery vs deployment Continuous delivery = every green build is automatically packaged and always ready to release; a human approves the prod push. Continuous deployment = that final push is automatic too — green reaches prod with no gate. The pipeline is identical up to the last step. Delivery","keywords":["delivery","vs","deployment"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-delivery.html#promotion","page":"Continuous delivery & deployment","section":"Promote one artifact across environments","tool":"cicd","text":"02 Promote one artifact across environments After CI, the same image walks through dev → staging → prod, passing gates. You don't rebuild per environment — you promote the identical bytes and only swap config. One artifact promoted to prod ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× One image from CI is deployed to st","keywords":["promote","one","artifact","across","environments"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-delivery.html#deploy-vs-release","page":"Continuous delivery & deployment","section":"Deploy is not release","tool":"cicd","text":"03 Deploy is not release Deploy = the new code is running on prod servers. Release = users can actually see it. A feature flag lets you deploy code \"dark\" and flip it on later — decoupling shipping risk from launch timing. 1 Deploy dark The new version is live in prod but the feature is behind an of","keywords":["deploy","is","not","release"]},{"url":"cicd/continuous-delivery.html#interview","page":"Continuous delivery & deployment","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"cicd","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire Continuous delivery vs continuous deployment? warm-up Model answer Both auto-build, auto-test, and auto-promote through environments so the artifact is always releasable. Delivery stops at a human approval for the production release; deployment automates that final step too —","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"containers/images-and-dockerfiles.html","page":"Images & Dockerfiles","section":"Images & Dockerfiles.","tool":"containers","text":"Images & Dockerfiles. An image is a sealed, layered snapshot of your app and everything it needs to run — built once, run identically on any host. The Dockerfile is the recipe; the build cache is what makes it fast.","keywords":["images","dockerfiles"]},{"url":"containers/images-and-dockerfiles.html#container-vs-vm","page":"Images & Dockerfiles","section":"Container vs VM","tool":"containers","text":"01 Container vs VM A VM virtualizes hardware and ships a whole guest OS; a container shares the host kernel and isolates only your process — so it's smaller, starts in milliseconds, and packs more densely. Containers get their isolation from two Linux kernel features: namespaces (each container sees","keywords":["container","vs","vm"]},{"url":"containers/images-and-dockerfiles.html#layers","page":"Images & Dockerfiles","section":"An image is a stack of layers","tool":"containers","text":"02 An image is a stack of layers Each Dockerfile instruction ( FROM , COPY , RUN ) produces one read-only layer . The final image is those layers stacked; a running container adds a thin writable layer on top. Layers are content-addressed and cached . Rebuild and only the layers whose inputs changed","keywords":["an","image","is","stack","of","layers","FROM","COPY","RUN"]},{"url":"containers/images-and-dockerfiles.html#dockerfile","page":"Images & Dockerfiles","section":"Dockerfile basics","tool":"containers","text":"03 Dockerfile basics A Dockerfile is an ordered list of instructions. Four carry most of the meaning: FROM The base image you build on. Sets the starting layers (OS + runtime). COPY Copy files from your build context into the image (e.g. source, deps manifest). RUN Execute a command at build time an","keywords":["dockerfile","basics","FROM","COPY","RUN","CMD","ENTRYPOINT","docker","run","args","python","app","py","img","bash","replaces","the","WHOLE","command","port","overrides","only","ARGS","SIGTERM"]},{"url":"containers/images-and-dockerfiles.html#multistage","page":"Images & Dockerfiles","section":"Multi-stage builds & small, secure bases","tool":"containers","text":"04 Multi-stage builds & small, secure bases A multi-stage build uses a fat stage to compile/install, then copies only the artifacts into a slim runtime stage — so compilers, build deps, and caches never ship to production. Dockerfile Copy # build stage (fat: has compilers) FROM python:3.12 AS build ","keywords":["multi","stage","builds","small","secure","bases","build","fat","has","compilers","FROM","python","AS","WORKDIR","app","COPY","requirements","txt","RUN","pip","install","prefix","runtime","slim","non","root","from","usr","local","USER","never","ENTRYPOINT","py"]},{"url":"containers/images-and-dockerfiles.html#cache-tags-secrets","page":"Images & Dockerfiles","section":"Cache order, tags vs digests, secrets","tool":"containers","text":"05 Cache order, tags vs digests, secrets Order instructions so dependencies cache before app code : copy the deps manifest and install first , then copy your fast-changing source last. Cache buster Dockerfile Copy COPY . . RUN pip install -r requirements.txt # any code edit busts the # install cache","keywords":["cache","order","tags","vs","digests","secrets","COPY","RUN","pip","install","requirements","txt","any","code","edit","busts","the","slow","rebuilds","edits","reuse","cached","layer","myapp","latest","sha256","f2","ENV","API_KEY","env"]},{"url":"containers/images-and-dockerfiles.html#interview","page":"Images & Dockerfiles","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"containers","text":"06 Interview rapid-fire Container vs VM — what's the real difference? warm-up Model answer A VM virtualizes hardware and runs a full guest OS on a hypervisor — heavy, slow to boot, strongly isolated. A container shares the host kernel and isolates the process with namespaces and cgroups — MBs not GB","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","COPY","from","slim","USER","sha256","latest"]},{"url":"containers/running-containers.html","page":"Running containers","section":"Running containers.","tool":"containers","text":"Running containers. Building an image is half the job; running it well is the other half. Where state lives, how config is injected, and what's allowed to disappear on restart decide whether your service survives a node dying at 3 a.m.","keywords":["running","containers"]},{"url":"containers/running-containers.html#registries","page":"Running containers","section":"Registries: push & pull","tool":"containers","text":"01 Registries: push & pull A registry stores and serves images. CI push es a built image; every host that runs it pull s it. The registry is the handoff point between build and run. On AWS the default is ECR (Elastic Container Registry) — private, IAM-controlled, in your account. Public options are ","keywords":["registries","push","pull","docker","compose","sha256"]},{"url":"containers/running-containers.html#run-knobs","page":"Running containers","section":"The knobs of docker run","tool":"containers","text":"02 The knobs of docker run Running a container means wiring it into the host: ports, storage, network, config, and a policy for when it dies. Ports -p 8080:80 maps a host port to the container port so traffic can reach it. Volumes -v data:/var/lib/app mounts persistent storage outside the container'","keywords":["the","knobs","of","docker","run","data","var","lib","app","LOG_LEVEL","info","GET","healthz","restart","on","failure","unless","stopped"]},{"url":"containers/running-containers.html#ephemeral","page":"Running containers","section":"Cattle, not pets: the ephemeral principle","tool":"containers","text":"03 Cattle, not pets: the ephemeral principle Treat containers as disposable . Anything written to the container's own filesystem is lost on restart — design so losing a container is a non-event. \"Pets\" are hand-fed, named, nursed back to health. \"Cattle\" are interchangeable and replaced without cere","keywords":["cattle","not","pets","the","ephemeral","principle","app","data"]},{"url":"containers/running-containers.html#compose","page":"Running containers","section":"Compose for local multi-service","tool":"containers","text":"04 Compose for local multi-service docker compose declares a multi-container app in one YAML file and brings it all up with docker compose up — great for local dev, not your production scheduler. compose.yaml Copy services: app: image: myapp@sha256:9f2... # pin by digest ports: [\"8080:8080\"] environ","keywords":["compose","for","local","multi","service","docker","up","services","app","image","myapp","sha256","f2","pin","by","digest","ports","environment","DB_URL","postgres","db","depends_on","volumes","pgdata","var","lib","postgresql","data"]},{"url":"containers/running-containers.html#where-to-run","page":"Running containers","section":"VM vs container vs serverless","tool":"containers","text":"05 VM vs container vs serverless The container isn't always the answer. Pick the compute model by how much control you need versus how much ops you want to own — and what idle costs you. EC2 gives the most control but the most ops and poor idle economics; containers (ECS/EKS) balance portability and","keywords":["vm","vs","container","serverless"]},{"url":"containers/running-containers.html#interview","page":"Running containers","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"containers","text":"06 Interview rapid-fire Where does container state go? warm-up Model answer Not in the container. The writable layer is ephemeral — it disappears when the container is replaced, rescheduled, or scaled in. State goes to a volume (for local persistence) or, in the cloud, an external store : S3 for fil","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","latest","sha256"]},{"url":"kubernetes/k8s-mental-model.html","page":"The Kubernetes mental model","section":"The Kubernetes mental model.","tool":"kubernetes","text":"The Kubernetes mental model. Kubernetes is one big control loop : you declare the state you want, and the cluster works nonstop to make reality match. You never run the steps yourself — you describe the destination.","keywords":["the","kubernetes","mental","model"]},{"url":"kubernetes/k8s-mental-model.html#control-loop","page":"The Kubernetes mental model","section":"Desired state, not steps","tool":"kubernetes","text":"01 Desired state, not steps Kubernetes is declarative : you POST the desired state (YAML), and controllers run a reconcile loop that continuously drives the actual state toward it. You don't say \"start container, then on crash restart it, then on node death reschedule it.\" You say \"I want 3 healthy ","keywords":["desired","state","not","steps","replicas"]},{"url":"kubernetes/k8s-mental-model.html#control-plane","page":"The Kubernetes mental model","section":"The control plane","tool":"kubernetes","text":"02 The control plane The control plane is the brain. You talk to exactly one part of it — the API server — and four components cooperate to turn your YAML into running containers. API server The front door — the only thing you (and every component) talk to. Validates requests, then reads/writes stat","keywords":["the","control","plane","containerd"]},{"url":"kubernetes/k8s-mental-model.html#apply-flow","page":"The Kubernetes mental model","section":"What happens on kubectl apply","tool":"kubernetes","text":"03 What happens on kubectl apply Walk one request end to end. The pattern — declare to the API server, persist in etcd, let controllers and the kubelet make it real — is the whole system in miniature. apply → store → schedule → run → reconcile ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× You apply YAML to the API serve","keywords":["what","happens","on","kubectl","apply","edit","scale","replicas"]},{"url":"kubernetes/k8s-mental-model.html#interview","page":"The Kubernetes mental model","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"kubernetes","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire Imperative vs declarative — what's the difference, and which is Kubernetes? warm-up Model answer Imperative = you issue the steps (\"create this, then scale to 3\"). Declarative = you describe the end state (\"I want 3 replicas\") and the system figures out how to get and stay th","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","apply"]},{"url":"kubernetes/core-objects.html","page":"Core objects","section":"Core Kubernetes objects.","tool":"kubernetes","text":"Core Kubernetes objects. A handful of objects compose into every app on Kubernetes: Pods run containers, Deployments keep them alive and roll them out, and Services give them a stable address. Learn how they nest and how traffic reaches a pod.","keywords":["core","kubernetes","objects"]},{"url":"kubernetes/core-objects.html#the-cast","page":"Core objects","section":"The cast of objects","tool":"kubernetes","text":"01 The cast of objects You mostly write a Deployment (it owns ReplicaSets → Pods) and a Service (a stable address in front of those pods). The rest — config, secrets, routing, namespaces — wrap around that core. In plain English analogy A Deployment is a shift manager who keeps N identical workers s","keywords":["the","cast","of","objects"]},{"url":"kubernetes/core-objects.html#deployment","page":"Core objects","section":"Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod","tool":"kubernetes","text":"02 Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod The nesting is the mental model: a Deployment creates a ReplicaSet, which creates Pods. The selector labels glue an object to the pods it owns or targets. deployment.yaml Copy apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: { name: web } spec: replicas: 3 # desired po","keywords":["deployment","replicaset","pod","selector","apiVersion","apps","v1","kind","Deployment","metadata","name","web","spec","replicas","desired","count","matchLabels","app","owns","pods","with","this","label","template","the","blueprint","labels","containers","image","ghcr","io","acme","pin","by","tag","SHA"]},{"url":"kubernetes/core-objects.html#traffic","page":"Core objects","section":"How traffic reaches a pod","tool":"kubernetes","text":"03 How traffic reaches a pod Pods are mortal and get fresh IPs, so clients never target them directly. A Service gives a stable address; an Ingress/Gateway handles HTTP routing from outside. Follow one request in. External request → Ingress → Service → Pod ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× A client hits the ","keywords":["how","traffic","reaches","pod","ClusterIP","NodePort","LoadBalancer","db","ns","svc","cluster","local"]},{"url":"kubernetes/core-objects.html#config-secrets","page":"Core objects","section":"Config vs secrets","tool":"kubernetes","text":"04 Config vs secrets Keep configuration out of the image so the same artifact runs everywhere. ConfigMap holds plain config; Secret holds sensitive values. Both inject into pods as env vars or mounted files. ConfigMap Non-secret: log level, feature flags, URLs. Stored as plain text in etcd. Safe to ","keywords":["config","vs","secrets","kubectl","describe"]},{"url":"kubernetes/core-objects.html#interview","page":"Core objects","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"kubernetes","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Pod vs Deployment vs Service — what does each do? warm-up Model answer A Pod is the smallest unit — one or more containers sharing a network and storage. A Deployment manages ReplicaSets to keep N pods running and to do rolling updates and rollbacks. A Service is a stable vir","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"kubernetes/workloads-and-scaling.html","page":"Workloads, scaling & config","section":"Workloads, health & scaling.","tool":"kubernetes","text":"Workloads, health & scaling. Once pods run, Kubernetes manages them for you: it rolls out new versions without downtime, probes health to restart or shield pods, packs them by resource requests , and autoscales to load. Pick the right workload type for the job.","keywords":["workloads","health","scaling"]},{"url":"kubernetes/workloads-and-scaling.html#rollouts","page":"Workloads, scaling & config","section":"Rolling updates & rollback","tool":"kubernetes","text":"01 Rolling updates & rollback A Deployment ships a new version by a rolling update : it swaps pods a few at a time, governed by maxSurge (extra pods allowed) and maxUnavailable (how many can be down) — so the Service never goes dark. In plain English analogy Resource requests are the table you reser","keywords":["rolling","updates","rollback","maxSurge","maxUnavailable","OOMKilled","kubectl","rollout","undo"]},{"url":"kubernetes/workloads-and-scaling.html#probes","page":"Workloads, scaling & config","section":"Health probes","tool":"kubernetes","text":"02 Health probes The kubelet checks each container with up to three probes. They answer different questions — confusing them causes outages. Liveness \"Is it dead/stuck ?\" Fails → restart the container . For deadlocks a restart can't-not-fix-itself out of. Readiness \"Can it serve right now ?\" Fails →","keywords":["health","probes","livenessProbe","restart","if","dead","httpGet","path","healthz","port","periodSeconds","readinessProbe","gate","traffic","until","ready","CrashLoopBackOff"]},{"url":"kubernetes/workloads-and-scaling.html#resources","page":"Workloads, scaling & config","section":"Requests, limits & autoscaling","tool":"kubernetes","text":"03 Requests, limits & autoscaling Requests guide scheduling (the seat you reserve); limits cap consumption (the hard ceiling). The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) then adds or removes replicas based on CPU or custom metrics. Requests — for the scheduler What the pod is guaranteed ; the scheduler sum","keywords":["requests","limits","autoscaling","OOMKilled","min","max"]},{"url":"kubernetes/workloads-and-scaling.html#workload-types","page":"Workloads, scaling & config","section":"Beyond Deployment","tool":"kubernetes","text":"04 Beyond Deployment Deployment is for stateless apps where any replica is interchangeable. When pods need identity, run per-node, or run-to-completion, reach for a different workload object. Workload Use it for Key property Deployment Stateless services (web/API). Interchangeable pods; easy rolling","keywords":["beyond","deployment","db","pg"]},{"url":"kubernetes/workloads-and-scaling.html#interview","page":"Workloads, scaling & config","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"kubernetes","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Liveness vs readiness probe — what's the difference? warm-up Model answer Liveness asks \"is it dead/stuck?\" — if it fails, Kubernetes restarts the container . Readiness asks \"can it serve right now?\" — if it fails, Kubernetes removes the pod from the Service (stops sending tr","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","OOMKilled","maxSurge","maxUnavailable","kubectl","rollout","undo"]},{"url":"iac/iac-and-terraform-model.html","page":"IaC & the Terraform model","section":"IaC & the Terraform model.","tool":"iac","text":"IaC & the Terraform model. Infrastructure as Code means your servers, networks, and databases are defined in version-controlled code and applied repeatably — not clicked together by hand. Terraform is the dominant tool, and interviewers probe how its plan / apply / state model actually works.","keywords":["iac","the","terraform","model"]},{"url":"iac/iac-and-terraform-model.html#what-is-iac","page":"IaC & the Terraform model","section":"Code, not clicks","tool":"iac","text":"01 Code, not clicks IaC = your infrastructure described in version-controlled code and provisioned repeatably by a tool. The opposite is ClickOps : building it by hand in the console — unrepeatable, undocumented, and impossible to review. The code is the single source of truth. To stand up an identi","keywords":["code","not","clicks"]},{"url":"iac/iac-and-terraform-model.html#declarative","page":"IaC & the Terraform model","section":"Declarative vs imperative","tool":"iac","text":"02 Declarative vs imperative Terraform is declarative : you describe the desired end state , and the tool computes the diff to get there. Imperative means you write the ordered steps yourself. Declarative is convergent — run it twice and the second run is a no-op, because reality already matches the","keywords":["declarative","vs","imperative","aws","ec2","run","instances"]},{"url":"iac/iac-and-terraform-model.html#terraform-model","page":"IaC & the Terraform model","section":"The Terraform model: plan → apply → state","tool":"iac","text":"03 The Terraform model: plan → apply → state Terraform connects to a cloud through a provider (e.g. aws ). You declare resources in HCL. terraform plan previews the exact diff; terraform apply makes it real and records what it built in the state file . main.tf Copy provider \"aws\" { region = \"us-east","keywords":["the","terraform","model","plan","apply","state","aws","provider","region","us","east","resource","aws_instance","web","ami","abc123","instance_type","t3","micro","tags","Name","tfstate"]},{"url":"iac/iac-and-terraform-model.html#ecosystem","page":"IaC & the Terraform model","section":"The ecosystem: Terraform, OpenTofu & AWS-native","tool":"iac","text":"04 The ecosystem: Terraform, OpenTofu & AWS-native Interviewers in 2026 expect you to know the license split . HashiCorp relicensed Terraform to the BSL (Business Source License) in 2023; IBM then acquired HashiCorp. The community forked the last open version into OpenTofu . Tool What it is License ","keywords":["the","ecosystem","terraform","opentofu","aws","native","plan","apply"]},{"url":"iac/iac-and-terraform-model.html#interview","page":"IaC & the Terraform model","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"iac","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Why use Infrastructure as Code instead of the console? warm-up Model answer Because IaC makes infrastructure repeatable, reviewable, and auditable . It lives in Git, so every change is a reviewed commit with history and rollback; you can stand up identical environments from t","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","terraform","tfstate","plan"]},{"url":"iac/terraform-in-practice.html","page":"Terraform in practice","section":"Terraform in practice.","tool":"iac","text":"Terraform in practice. Knowing plan and apply isn't enough — interviewers want the team workflow : reusable modules, remote locked state, multiple environments, drift, importing existing infra, and running it all in CI as GitOps for infrastructure.","keywords":["terraform","in","practice"]},{"url":"iac/terraform-in-practice.html#modules","page":"Terraform in practice","section":"Modules: functions for infrastructure","tool":"iac","text":"01 Modules: functions for infrastructure A module is reusable, parameterized infrastructure — write the pattern once (a VPC, an ECS service) and call it with different inputs . Variables are its parameters; outputs are its return values. Modules kill copy-paste. One audited \"service\" module, called ","keywords":["modules","functions","for","infrastructure","module","api","source","service","name","cpu","512","desired_count","output","api_url","value","url"]},{"url":"iac/terraform-in-practice.html#remote-state","page":"Terraform in practice","section":"Remote state & locking","tool":"iac","text":"02 Remote state & locking Store state remotely (S3 + a DynamoDB lock table, or Terraform Cloud) so the whole team shares one source of truth. State locking stops two concurrent apply s from corrupting it. Local state on one laptop is a disaster waiting to happen: nobody else can apply, the file gets","keywords":["remote","state","locking","apply","terraform","backend","s3","bucket","acme","tfstate","key","prod","region","us","east","dynamodb_table","tf","locks","encrypt","true","at","rest"]},{"url":"iac/terraform-in-practice.html#environments","page":"Terraform in practice","section":"Multiple environments & drift","tool":"iac","text":"03 Multiple environments & drift Each environment (dev/staging/prod) needs its own state so a bad prod apply can't touch dev. Two common patterns: Per-env directories Separate folders ( envs/prod , envs/staging ), each with its own backend state, all calling the same modules. Explicit, recommended f","keywords":["multiple","environments","drift","envs","prod","staging","terraform","workspace","plan","import","aws_instance","web","abc123"]},{"url":"iac/terraform-in-practice.html#terraform-in-ci","page":"Terraform in practice","section":"Terraform in CI: GitOps for infra","tool":"iac","text":"04 Terraform in CI: GitOps for infra Run Terraform in a pipeline, not from laptops: plan on the pull request so reviewers see the exact diff, and apply on merge to main. Git becomes the source of truth for infrastructure. 1 Write Open a PR changing the HCL. The change is now reviewable code, not a c","keywords":["terraform","in","ci","gitops","for","infra","plan","apply","destroy"]},{"url":"iac/terraform-in-practice.html#interview","page":"Terraform in practice","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"iac","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire How do you manage Terraform state for a team, and what is state locking? core Model answer Use a remote backend — typically S3 with encryption for the state, plus a DynamoDB table for locking (or Terraform Cloud). Everyone reads and writes the same shared, versioned state. St","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","apply","terraform","force","unlock","plan","import"]},{"url":"aws/compute-and-networking.html","page":"AWS compute & networking","section":"Compute & networking.","tool":"aws","text":"Compute & networking. EC2 instances are the raw compute; an Auto Scaling Group keeps the right number of healthy ones; a load balancer fans traffic to them; and a VPC is the private network they all live in. This is the backbone every AWS architecture question circles back to.","keywords":["compute","networking"]},{"url":"aws/compute-and-networking.html#ec2","page":"AWS compute & networking","section":"EC2: instances, AMIs & families","tool":"aws","text":"01 EC2: instances, AMIs & families An EC2 instance is a virtual server you rent by the second. You boot it from an AMI (a machine image — OS + your baked-in software) and pick an instance type from a family sized for your workload. Families map to a shape: t/m general-purpose, c compute-heavy, r/x m","keywords":["ec2","instances","amis","families","large","xlarge"]},{"url":"aws/compute-and-networking.html#asg-lb","page":"AWS compute & networking","section":"Auto Scaling Groups & load balancers","tool":"aws","text":"02 Auto Scaling Groups & load balancers An ASG keeps your fleet between min and max , tracking a desired count. Scaling policies grow or shrink it to match load, and it self-heals — a failed health check means the instance is killed and replaced automatically. min / desired / max Floor, current targ","keywords":["auto","scaling","groups","load","balancers","api","shop","example","com"]},{"url":"aws/compute-and-networking.html#vpc","page":"AWS compute & networking","section":"VPC: subnets, routing & egress","tool":"aws","text":"03 VPC: subnets, routing & egress A VPC is a private network defined by a CIDR block (e.g. 10.0.0.0/16 ), carved into subnets across multiple AZs. Public subnets have a route to the Internet Gateway ; private subnets do not — they reach the internet outbound only through a NAT gateway . A request in","keywords":["vpc","subnets","routing","egress"]},{"url":"aws/compute-and-networking.html#security","page":"AWS compute & networking","section":"Security groups vs NACLs","tool":"aws","text":"04 Security groups vs NACLs Two firewall layers. A security group wraps an instance/ENI and is stateful — allow traffic out and the reply is automatically allowed back. A NACL guards a whole subnet and is stateless — you must allow both directions explicitly. Dimension Security group NACL Attaches t","keywords":["security","groups","vs","nacls","pip","install"]},{"url":"aws/compute-and-networking.html#sizing","page":"AWS compute & networking","section":"Sizing the web tier","tool":"aws","text":"05 Sizing the web tier Interviewers love \"how many instances?\" The answer is back-of-envelope: turn users into peak QPS , divide by what one server handles, and round up. Drag the sliders to feel how traffic drives fleet size. For ~2M daily users at 5 writes each with a 20:1 read:write ratio, averag","keywords":["sizing","the","web","tier"]},{"url":"aws/compute-and-networking.html#interview","page":"AWS compute & networking","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"aws","text":"06 Interview rapid-fire ALB vs NLB — when do you reach for each? warm-up Model answer The ALB is a Layer-7 (HTTP) load balancer: it routes by host and path , terminates TLS, and speaks WebSockets/gRPC — my default for web apps and microservices. The NLB is Layer-4 (TCP/UDP): no HTTP awareness, but e","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"aws/containers-and-serverless.html","page":"AWS containers & serverless","section":"Containers & serverless.","tool":"aws","text":"Containers & serverless. Once you have a container image, AWS gives you three places to run code: ECS/Fargate (AWS-native containers), EKS (managed Kubernetes), and Lambda (event-driven functions). The interview test is knowing which to reach for — and why.","keywords":["containers","serverless"]},{"url":"aws/containers-and-serverless.html#ecs-fargate","page":"AWS containers & serverless","section":"ECS & Fargate: containers, AWS-native","tool":"aws","text":"01 ECS & Fargate: containers, AWS-native ECS is AWS's own container orchestrator — it schedules and runs your containers. Fargate is a launch mode where AWS runs them for you with no servers to manage ; you just declare CPU/memory per task. Images come from ECR , AWS's private registry. In plain Eng","keywords":["ecs","fargate","containers","aws","native","family","web","requiresCompatibilities","FARGATE","cpu","512","memory","containerDefinitions","name","app","image","dkr","ecr","us","east","amazonaws","com","sha256","f2","portMappings","containerPort"]},{"url":"aws/containers-and-serverless.html#eks","page":"AWS containers & serverless","section":"EKS: managed Kubernetes","tool":"aws","text":"02 EKS: managed Kubernetes EKS is AWS-managed Kubernetes — AWS runs the control plane; you get the full K8s API and its entire ecosystem. Reach for it when you want portability or the K8s tooling (Helm, operators, GitOps with Argo/Flux), or you already run Kubernetes. The trade is power versus effor","keywords":["eks","managed","kubernetes"]},{"url":"aws/containers-and-serverless.html#lambda","page":"AWS containers & serverless","section":"Lambda: event-driven functions","tool":"aws","text":"03 Lambda: event-driven functions Lambda runs a function in response to an event — no servers, pay per invocation , scales to zero. You write the handler; AWS runs it when a trigger fires. Triggers are the point: a function wires to an event source and runs only when something happens. Triggers → La","keywords":["lambda","event","driven","functions"]},{"url":"aws/containers-and-serverless.html#decision","page":"AWS containers & serverless","section":"ECS vs EKS vs Lambda: the decision","tool":"aws","text":"04 ECS vs EKS vs Lambda: the decision Decide on four axes: ops burden , portability , cost model , and latency / cold-start . Most teams in 2026 default to ECS/Fargate , add EKS when they need the K8s ecosystem, and sprinkle Lambda for glue and spiky events. Dimension ECS / Fargate EKS (Kubernetes) ","keywords":["ecs","vs","eks","lambda","the","decision"]},{"url":"aws/containers-and-serverless.html#interview","page":"AWS containers & serverless","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"aws","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire ECS vs EKS — when do you choose each? core Model answer Both run containers; the difference is the API and the ops burden. ECS is AWS-native and operationally light — great when you're all-in on AWS and don't need Kubernetes. EKS is managed Kubernetes: pick it when you want t","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"aws/iam-storage-data.html","page":"IAM, storage & data","section":"IAM, storage & data.","tool":"aws","text":"IAM, storage & data. Who can do what (IAM), where bytes live (S3, EBS, EFS), where your data sits (RDS/Aurora), and how secrets and encryption are handled (Secrets Manager, SSM, KMS). This is the security-and-state backbone interviewers probe hardest.","keywords":["iam","storage","data"]},{"url":"aws/iam-storage-data.html#iam","page":"IAM, storage & data","section":"IAM: users, roles & least privilege","tool":"aws","text":"01 IAM: users, roles & least privilege An IAM user is a permanent identity with long-lived credentials. A role is assumed temporarily and yields short-lived credentials . Strongly prefer roles. Permissions come from policies — JSON allow/deny statements — and you grant the least privilege needed. In","keywords":["iam","users","roles","least","privilege","AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY","Version","Statement","Effect","Allow","Action","s3","GetObject","PutObject","Resource","arn","aws","acme","uploads"]},{"url":"aws/iam-storage-data.html#storage","page":"IAM, storage & data","section":"Storage: S3 vs EBS vs EFS","tool":"aws","text":"02 Storage: S3 vs EBS vs EFS Three storage shapes for three different jobs. The interview question is almost always \"which one, and why?\" Service Type Attaches to Use it for S3 Object store (buckets/keys) Anything, over HTTPS Files, backups, static assets, data lakes, artifacts. EBS Block volume (a ","keywords":["storage","s3","vs","ebs","efs"]},{"url":"aws/iam-storage-data.html#data-secrets","page":"IAM, storage & data","section":"Data, secrets & encryption","tool":"aws","text":"03 Data, secrets & encryption RDS / Aurora are managed relational databases — AWS runs the engine, patching, and backups. Turn on Multi-AZ for high availability: a standby in another Availability Zone takes over on failure. Secrets Manager Stores secrets (DB passwords, API keys) with automatic rotat","keywords":["data","secrets","encryption","SecureString"]},{"url":"aws/iam-storage-data.html#interview","page":"IAM, storage & data","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"aws","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire IAM user vs role — and how does a workload get credentials without keys? core Model answer A user is a permanent identity with long-lived keys; a role is assumed temporarily and gives short-lived credentials — prefer roles. Workloads get creds with no keys by assuming a role ","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","SecureString"]},{"url":"delivery/release-strategies.html","page":"Release strategies","section":"Release strategies.","tool":"delivery","text":"Release strategies. Once CI hands you a tested image, how you swap it into production decides whether a bad version takes everyone down or a handful of requests. Each strategy is a different bet on blast radius, cost, and rollback speed.","keywords":["release","strategies"]},{"url":"delivery/release-strategies.html#map","page":"Release strategies","section":"Five ways to ship v2","tool":"delivery","text":"01 Five ways to ship v2 The whole game is blast radius vs cost vs rollback speed . Recreate is cheapest and worst; canary is safest; blue-green is instant but doubles capacity; feature flags split deploy from release entirely. The artifact is identical across all of them — what changes is how traffi","keywords":["five","ways","to","ship","v2"]},{"url":"delivery/release-strategies.html#rollback","page":"Release strategies","section":"The rollback story per strategy","tool":"delivery","text":"02 The rollback story per strategy Interviewers care less about how you ship than how you un-ship . Each strategy has a very different \"undo\" — and that, not deploy speed, is usually the deciding factor. Strategy Rollback move Speed Cost / caveat Recreate Redeploy v1 (stop all, start old) Slow + ano","keywords":["the","rollback","story","per","strategy"]},{"url":"delivery/release-strategies.html#deploy-vs-release","page":"Release strategies","section":"Deploy ≠ release: where flags fit","tool":"delivery","text":"03 Deploy ≠ release: where flags fit Deploy = new code is running on prod servers. Release = users can see it. Blue-green and canary move traffic ; a feature flag moves visibility — and they compose. You can deploy a new version via rolling update with every new feature behind an off flag, then \"rel","keywords":["deploy","release","where","flags","fit"]},{"url":"delivery/release-strategies.html#db-migrations","page":"Release strategies","section":"The database makes \"zero-downtime\" hard","tool":"delivery","text":"04 The database makes \"zero-downtime\" hard Code rolls back in seconds; a schema change usually can't . During any gradual rollout, old and new code hit the same database at once — so the schema must satisfy both. The fix is expand → migrate → contract . 1 Expand Add the new column/table additively —","keywords":["the","database","makes","zero","downtime","hard"]},{"url":"delivery/release-strategies.html#interview","page":"Release strategies","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"delivery","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Blue-green vs canary — when do you pick which? core Model answer Blue-green runs two full environments and flips all traffic at once — instant cutover and instant rollback, at the cost of ~2× capacity. Canary shifts a small percentage first and widens gradually while you watc","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"delivery/gitops-progressive-delivery.html","page":"GitOps & progressive delivery","section":"GitOps & progressive delivery.","tool":"delivery","text":"GitOps & progressive delivery. Stop running kubectl apply from a laptop or a CI step holding cluster keys. With GitOps, a Git repo is the single source of truth for what should be running, and an in-cluster agent continuously makes reality match it.","keywords":["gitops","progressive","delivery"]},{"url":"delivery/gitops-progressive-delivery.html#idea","page":"GitOps & progressive delivery","section":"What GitOps actually is","tool":"delivery","text":"01 What GitOps actually is GitOps = Git holds the desired state of the cluster, and an in-cluster agent ( Argo CD or Flux ) continuously reconciles the live cluster to match it — auto-healing any drift. Deploys become git commits; rollbacks become git revert . You never push to the cluster. You chan","keywords":["what","gitops","actually","is","git","revert","kubectl","edit"]},{"url":"delivery/gitops-progressive-delivery.html#reconcile","page":"GitOps & progressive delivery","section":"The reconcile loop","tool":"delivery","text":"02 The reconcile loop Commit to the config repo → the agent detects a diff → it syncs the cluster → cluster matches Git. Same loop catches and heals drift : tamper with the cluster directly and it gets pulled back. GitOps: commit → sync → drift → heal ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× A merge updates the des","keywords":["the","reconcile","loop"]},{"url":"delivery/gitops-progressive-delivery.html#push-vs-pull","page":"GitOps & progressive delivery","section":"Pull vs push delivery","tool":"delivery","text":"03 Pull vs push delivery Pull (GitOps): an agent inside the cluster pulls from Git — no external system holds cluster credentials. Push : CI reaches into the cluster with kubeconfig/keys and applies. Pull wins on security, audit, and drift; push wins on initial simplicity. Higher credential exposure","keywords":["pull","vs","push","delivery"]},{"url":"delivery/gitops-progressive-delivery.html#ci-vs-gitops","page":"GitOps & progressive delivery","section":"CI builds, GitOps deploys + progressive delivery","tool":"delivery","text":"04 CI builds, GitOps deploys + progressive delivery Clean division of labor: CI builds and pushes the image; it then writes the new image tag into the config repo . GitOps sees that commit and deploys it. Add Argo Rollouts / Flagger and the agent automates canary/blue-green with promotion gates. Whe","keywords":["ci","builds","gitops","deploys","progressive","delivery","kubectl","apply"]},{"url":"delivery/gitops-progressive-delivery.html#interview","page":"GitOps & progressive delivery","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"delivery","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What is GitOps, in one breath? warm-up Model answer GitOps means a Git repo holds the declarative desired state of your infrastructure, and an in-cluster agent like Argo CD or Flux continuously reconciles the live cluster to match it. You deploy by committing a manifest chang","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","kubectl","git","revert"]},{"url":"operate/incident-response.html","page":"Incident response & on-call","section":"Incident response.","tool":"operate","text":"Incident response. When prod breaks, calm process beats heroics. One person leads, the team triages by severity, and you stop the bleeding before you diagnose the disease — because every minute of outage is users and money.","keywords":["incident","response"]},{"url":"operate/incident-response.html#idea","page":"Incident response & on-call","section":"The ER model","tool":"operate","text":"01 The ER model An incident is any unplanned disruption to a service. The response is a structured drill : detect, declare, assign a leader, mitigate, resolve, then learn — not a free-for-all on a 30-person call. The job in the moment is not to be clever; it's to restore service fast and keep everyo","keywords":["the","er","model"]},{"url":"operate/incident-response.html#severity","page":"Incident response & on-call","section":"Severity levels","tool":"operate","text":"02 Severity levels Severity sizes the response: who wakes up, how fast, and how loudly you communicate. A SEV1 pulls in leadership and a status-page update; a SEV3 can wait for business hours. Severity What it means Response expectation SEV1 Major outage — service down or data at risk for many users","keywords":["severity","levels"]},{"url":"operate/incident-response.html#roles","page":"Incident response & on-call","section":"The Incident Commander & crew","tool":"operate","text":"03 The Incident Commander & crew The Incident Commander (IC) coordinates — they do not fix . They own decisions and the timeline, freeing the responders to actually debug. One throat to choke, one brain not buried in logs. Incident Commander Runs the response: drives priorities, decides to mitigate/","keywords":["the","incident","commander","crew"]},{"url":"operate/incident-response.html#comms-outcomes","page":"Incident response & on-call","section":"Communication & the clock","tool":"operate","text":"04 Communication & the clock Clear, frequent updates buy trust even while you're still fixing. Post short status-page notes (\"investigating → identified → monitoring → resolved\") on a cadence; silence reads as \"they don't know it's broken.\" MTTA Mean time to acknowledge — page fired → human owns it.","keywords":["communication","the","clock"]},{"url":"operate/incident-response.html#interview","page":"Incident response & on-call","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"operate","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What does an Incident Commander actually do? warm-up Model answer The IC coordinates the response, they don't fix the bug . They own decisions (mitigate now? roll back?), set the one current priority, delegate tasks, shield responders from noise, and keep the timeline. The po","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"operate/postmortems-and-resilience.html","page":"Postmortems & resilience","section":"Postmortems & resilience.","tool":"operate","text":"Postmortems & resilience. The incident is over — now extract the lesson and build the system that survives the next one. Blameless postmortems turn outages into upgrades, and resilience patterns make failure boring instead of catastrophic.","keywords":["postmortems","resilience"]},{"url":"operate/postmortems-and-resilience.html#blameless","page":"Postmortems & resilience","section":"Blameless postmortems","tool":"operate","text":"01 Blameless postmortems A postmortem documents an incident to prevent recurrence, not assign blame . It captures a timeline, impact, root cause, and action items with owners + due dates — and focuses on the systems and contributing factors, never the individual. \"Blameless\" is not \"no accountabilit","keywords":["blameless","postmortems"]},{"url":"operate/postmortems-and-resilience.html#resilience","page":"Postmortems & resilience","section":"Resilience patterns","tool":"operate","text":"02 Resilience patterns Resilience means a single failure degrades gracefully instead of cascading. These patterns are the standard toolkit — most are a few lines of client config, not a rewrite. Timeouts Never wait forever on a dependency. A request with no timeout ties up a thread until the whole s","keywords":["resilience","patterns","import","random","time","def","backoff","attempt","base","cap","exponential","window","then","pick","RANDOM","point","in","it","jitter","min","return","uniform","for","range","if","call_dependency","break","sleep","spread","de","sync","retries"]},{"url":"operate/postmortems-and-resilience.html#chaos","page":"Postmortems & resilience","section":"Chaos engineering","tool":"operate","text":"03 Chaos engineering Chaos engineering is deliberately injecting failure — killing instances, adding latency, blocking a dependency — to find weaknesses before they find you , in a controlled experiment with a hypothesis and a blast-radius limit. A game day is the human version: schedule a planned f","keywords":["chaos","engineering"]},{"url":"operate/postmortems-and-resilience.html#dr","page":"Postmortems & resilience","section":"Backups & disaster recovery","tool":"operate","text":"04 Backups & disaster recovery DR planning hinges on two numbers that the business — not the engineer — sets. They drive how much you spend on replication and standby capacity. RTO — Recovery Time Objective How fast you must recover. \"Back up within 1 hour.\" Lower RTO → warmer standby (and more cost","keywords":["backups","disaster","recovery"]},{"url":"operate/postmortems-and-resilience.html#interview","page":"Postmortems & resilience","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"operate","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Why run blameless postmortems? warm-up Model answer Because the goal is to prevent the next outage, not punish this one. Blame makes people hide details, so you find the wrong root cause and the failure recurs. Going blameless gets the honest timeline, points the fix at the s","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"operate/devsecops-essentials.html","page":"DevSecOps essentials","section":"DevSecOps essentials.","tool":"operate","text":"DevSecOps essentials. Security isn't a gate at the end — it's baked into the pipeline. Scan early, store secrets safely, grant least privilege, and prove what you ship — so vulnerabilities die in CI, not in a breach report.","keywords":["devsecops","essentials"]},{"url":"operate/devsecops-essentials.html#shift-left","page":"DevSecOps essentials","section":"Shift-left security","tool":"operate","text":"01 Shift-left security \"Shift left\" means moving security checks earlier — into the developer's PR and the CI run — instead of a security review the week before launch. The earlier a flaw is caught, the cheaper and safer it is to fix. It's the same logic as CI testing: a vulnerable dependency flagge","keywords":["shift","left","security"]},{"url":"operate/devsecops-essentials.html#secrets","page":"DevSecOps essentials","section":"Secrets management","tool":"operate","text":"02 Secrets management Secrets never belong in Git or in an image. Store them in a vault — AWS Secrets Manager / SSM Parameter Store or HashiCorp Vault — and inject them at runtime. Better still: skip long-lived keys entirely with short-lived credentials via OIDC . Anti-pattern AWS keys hardcoded in ","keywords":["secrets","management","env","AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY","git","rm"]},{"url":"operate/devsecops-essentials.html#least-privilege-scanning","page":"DevSecOps essentials","section":"Least privilege & scanning","tool":"operate","text":"03 Least privilege & scanning Two foundations that limit damage: grant only the permissions actually needed, and let machines find known vulnerabilities for you — and block on the bad ones. Least privilege Every identity gets the minimum it needs — scoped IAM policies, K8s RBAC roles, one role per w","keywords":["least","privilege","scanning","npm","audit","pip","name","Scan","image","with","Trivy","uses","aquasecurity","trivy","action","v0","ref","myapp","github","sha","severity","CRITICAL","HIGH","exit","code","fail","the","build","on","hit","ignore","unfixed","true","don","block","CVEs","no","patch","yet"]},{"url":"operate/devsecops-essentials.html#supply-chain","page":"DevSecOps essentials","section":"Supply-chain security","tool":"operate","text":"04 Supply-chain security You don't just ship your code — you ship every dependency and base image too. Supply-chain controls answer \" what exactly is in this artifact, and can I prove it's the one we built? \" SBOM Software Bill of Materials — a manifest of every component/version in the artifact (fo","keywords":["supply","chain","security","sha256","latest"]},{"url":"operate/devsecops-essentials.html#interview","page":"DevSecOps essentials","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"operate","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Where do secrets belong, and why prefer short-lived credentials? warm-up Model answer In a secrets manager — AWS Secrets Manager /SSM or Vault — injected at runtime, never in Git or baked into an image. Prefer short-lived creds (OIDC-issued tokens, IAM role assumption) over l","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"interview/index.html","page":"How DevOps interviews work","section":"How DevOps interviews work.","tool":"interview","text":"How DevOps interviews work. A DevOps loop tests less \"do you know the flag\" and more \"can you reason about a system under pressure.\" This hub gives you the rounds, the shape of a strong answer, and the practice surfaces to drill them.","keywords":["how","devops","interviews","work"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#rounds","page":"How DevOps interviews work","section":"The rounds, and what each probes","tool":"interview","text":"01 The rounds, and what each probes Most DevOps loops in 2026 run 4–5 rounds . The early ones check fundamentals ; the later ones check judgement under ambiguity — trade-offs, failure, and how you communicate. Round What it looks like What it's really scoring (basic → pro) Recruiter screen 15–30 min","keywords":["the","rounds","and","what","each","probes"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#answer-shape","page":"How DevOps interviews work","section":"The shape of a strong answer","tool":"interview","text":"02 The shape of a strong answer Weak answers stop at the definition. Strong answers follow a four-beat formula that signals real experience in about three sentences. 1 Define the term One precise sentence. Show you actually know what it is, not just that you've heard it. 2 Give a concrete example or","keywords":["the","shape","of","strong","answer","Deployment"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#map","page":"How DevOps interviews work","section":"Your practice surfaces","tool":"interview","text":"03 Your practice surfaces Work through these in order, or jump to whatever's weakest. Read for understanding, then close the page and recall out loud — recall beats re-reading. Question bank 50+ Q&A across culture, Linux, CI/CD, Docker, K8s, Terraform, AWS & delivery — with model answers. Scenario w","keywords":["your","practice","surfaces"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#interview","page":"How DevOps interviews work","section":"Interview technique itself","tool":"interview","text":"04 Interview technique itself How do you approach a question you don't know the answer to? core Model answer Say what you do know and reason out loud from first principles — interviewers score the thinking, not just the recall. State your assumptions explicitly (\"I haven't used X, but it sounds like","keywords":["interview","technique","itself"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"The DevOps question bank.","tool":"interview","text":"The DevOps question bank. 50+ of the questions that actually come up in DevOps loops, grouped by theme, each with a tight model answer. Read the answer, then close the page and say it back in your own words.","keywords":["the","devops","question","bank"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#culture","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"DevOps culture, DORA & SRE","tool":"interview","text":"01 DevOps culture, DORA & SRE What is DevOps, in one breath? warm-up Model answer A culture and set of practices that shorten and de-risk the loop from writing code to running it in production — through shared ownership, automation, and fast feedback. Summarized by CALMS (Culture, Automation, Lean, ","keywords":["devops","culture","dora","sre"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#linux-net","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"Linux & networking","tool":"interview","text":"02 Linux & networking A Linux box is out of disk. How do you find what's eating it? core Model answer Start with df -h to see which mount is full, then du -sh /* (or du -xh / | sort -h | tail ) to drill into the biggest directories. Watch for the classic trap: a deleted-but-still-open file held by a","keywords":["linux","networking","df","du","sh","xh","sort","tail","lsof","grep","deleted","chmod","644","755","600","ps","systemctl","status","ss","ltnp","curl","localhost"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#cicd","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"Git & CI/CD","tool":"interview","text":"03 Git & CI/CD CI vs CD vs continuous deployment? warm-up Model answer CI = every change is auto-built and tested against main. Continuous delivery = every green build is auto-packaged and always ready to release, but a human approves prod. Continuous deployment = that final push is automatic too — ","keywords":["git","ci","cd","main","filter","repo","AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#containers","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"Containers (Docker)","tool":"interview","text":"04 Containers (Docker) Container vs VM — what's the real difference? warm-up Model answer A VM virtualizes hardware and runs a full guest OS with its own kernel — heavy, slow to boot, strong isolation. A container virtualizes the OS : processes share the host kernel, isolated by namespaces and cgrou","keywords":["containers","docker","COPY","from","slim","USER","run","RUN","dockerignore","git"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#kubernetes","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"Kubernetes","tool":"interview","text":"05 Kubernetes Explain Kubernetes in one sentence. warm-up Model answer A control loop that continuously drives the cluster's actual state toward a declared desired state — you describe what you want (3 replicas of this image), and controllers reconcile reality to match, rescheduling on failure. It's","keywords":["kubernetes"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#iac","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"Infrastructure as Code (Terraform)","tool":"interview","text":"06 Infrastructure as Code (Terraform) What problem does Infrastructure as Code solve? warm-up Model answer It replaces click-ops and snowflake servers with version-controlled, reviewable, repeatable infra definitions. You get the same environment every time, a diff before you change anything, and an","keywords":["infrastructure","as","code","terraform","plan","apply","import","tfvars"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#aws","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"AWS for DevOps","tool":"interview","text":"07 AWS for DevOps Security group vs NACL? core Model answer A security group is stateful and attached to an ENI/instance — allow rules only; return traffic is auto-permitted. A NACL is stateless and attached to a subnet — explicit allow and deny, evaluated by rule number, and you must allow return t","keywords":["aws","for","devops"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#delivery","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"Deploy strategies & GitOps","tool":"interview","text":"08 Deploy strategies & GitOps Compare blue-green and canary deployments. core Model answer Blue-green stands up a full second environment, then flips all traffic at once — instant rollback (flip back), but double the infra. Canary shifts a small % to the new version, watches health, and widens gradu","keywords":["deploy","strategies","gitops"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#interview","page":"DevOps question bank","section":"Reliability, security & behavioral","tool":"interview","text":"09 Reliability, security & behavioral Walk me through how you'd handle a production incident. core Model answer Declare the incident and assign an incident commander to coordinate. Prioritize mitigation over root cause — roll back, flip a flag, or scale out to stop the bleeding first. Communicate st","keywords":["reliability","security","behavioral"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios.html","page":"Scenario walkthroughs","section":"Scenario walkthroughs.","tool":"interview","text":"Scenario walkthroughs. The open-ended design and debugging prompts. For each one: what the interviewer asks, how to clarify before you dive in, the approach, and exactly what a strong answer lands. Reason out loud — that's most of the score.","keywords":["scenario","walkthroughs"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios.html#zero-downtime","page":"Scenario walkthroughs","section":"Design a zero-downtime deploy on AWS","tool":"interview","text":"01 Design a zero-downtime deploy on AWS Prompt: \"We deploy a service on AWS and users see errors during every release. Design a zero-downtime deployment.\" Clarify first Where does it run — EC2 + ASG, ECS/Fargate, or EKS? (Decides the rollout mechanism.) Is it behind an ALB with health checks already","keywords":["design","zero","downtime","deploy","on","aws"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios.html#pipeline","page":"Scenario walkthroughs","section":"Build a CI/CD pipeline from scratch","tool":"interview","text":"02 Build a CI/CD pipeline from scratch Prompt: \"Design a CI/CD pipeline from scratch for a containerized web app.\" Clarify first Language/framework, and where it deploys — ECS/Fargate, EKS, or Lambda? Which CI tool, or is it open? (GitHub Actions is a safe default in 2026.) Branching model — trunk-b","keywords":["build","ci","cd","pipeline","from","scratch"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios.html#stuck-rollout","page":"Scenario walkthroughs","section":"Triage a stuck rollout / CrashLoopBackOff","tool":"interview","text":"03 Triage a stuck rollout / CrashLoopBackOff Prompt: \"A Kubernetes rollout is stuck and pods are in CrashLoopBackOff . Walk me through triaging it.\" This scores your method , not memorized fixes. Clarify first What changed in this release — image tag, config, resources, or manifest? Is it all new po","keywords":["triage","stuck","rollout","crashloopbackoff","CrashLoopBackOff","kubectl","status","deploy","app","get","pods","describe","pod","ImagePullBackOff","OOMKilled","logs","previous","undo"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios.html#aws-infra","page":"Scenario walkthroughs","section":"Stand up production infra on AWS","tool":"interview","text":"04 Stand up production infra on AWS Prompt: \"Stand up production infrastructure for a web app on AWS.\" They want a resilient, secure, reproducible baseline — and to hear you say IaC and Multi-AZ unprompted. Clarify first Traffic shape and expected scale? Stateless web tier, or stateful components to","keywords":["stand","up","production","infra","on","aws"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios.html#interview","page":"Scenario walkthroughs","section":"What interviewers are really scoring","tool":"interview","text":"05 What interviewers are really scoring Across every scenario above, the rubric is the same. The \"right answer\" matters less than demonstrating these five behaviors out loud. Clarifies requirements Asks about scale, stack, constraints, and rollback appetite before designing. Jumping straight to a so","keywords":["what","interviewers","are","really","scoring"]},{"url":"interview/cheat-sheet.html","page":"DevOps cheat-sheet","section":"The night-before cheat-sheet.","tool":"interview","text":"The night-before cheat-sheet. Dense, rapid-recall reference across the whole stack — Git and containers, Kubernetes and Terraform, AWS, deploy strategies and incident basics. Skim it the night before; recall it in the room.","keywords":["the","night","before","cheat","sheet"]},{"url":"interview/cheat-sheet.html#build-ship","page":"DevOps cheat-sheet","section":"Build & ship","tool":"interview","text":"01 Build & ship The inner loop: Git hygiene, a tiny secure image , and a CI pipeline that keeps main always releasable. Git essentials Rebase vs merge Rebase = linear history, rewrites commits. Merge = preserves both branches with a merge commit. Undo a commit git revert (safe, new commit) on shared","keywords":["build","ship","main","git","revert","reset","feat","fix","chore","RUN","CMD","ENTRYPOINT","COPY","ADD","slim","USER","FROM","python","AS","src","pip","install","target","out","requirements","txt","gcr","io","distroless","python3","from","app","py"]},{"url":"interview/cheat-sheet.html#orchestrate-provision","page":"DevOps cheat-sheet","section":"Orchestrate & provision","tool":"interview","text":"02 Orchestrate & provision Kubernetes runs the containers; Terraform provisions the world they run in. Both are declarative — you describe the desired state. kubectl quick hits Command Use get pods -o wide List pods + node/IP. describe pod X Events — why it won't start. logs -f X Tail container logs","keywords":["orchestrate","provision","get","pods","wide","describe","pod","logs","exec","it","sh","rollout","status","deploy","undo","apply","etcd","kubelet","liveness","readiness","terraform","init","download","providers","backend","plan","diff","desired","vs","current","state","make","so","destroy","tear","down"]},{"url":"interview/cheat-sheet.html#cloud-operate","page":"DevOps cheat-sheet","section":"Cloud & operate","tool":"interview","text":"03 Cloud & operate The AWS building blocks, how you ship safely, and how you measure and respond when it breaks. AWS service map Need Service VMs + scaling EC2 + Auto Scaling Group Containers ECS / EKS, Fargate (serverless), ECR (registry) Functions Lambda + API Gateway, EventBridge Load balance ALB","keywords":["cloud","operate"]},{"url":"interview/flashcards.html","page":"DevOps flashcards","section":"Active-recall flashcards.","tool":"interview","text":"Active-recall flashcards. Quiz yourself across every module — CI/CD, Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, AWS, deploy strategies, GitOps, reliability, and DevSecOps. Tap a card to flip; mark it Known or Review. Shuffle and repeat until it sticks.","keywords":["active","recall","flashcards"]},{"url":"interview/flashcards.html#cards","page":"DevOps flashcards","section":"The deck","tool":"interview","text":"01 The deck Recall beats re-reading. Try to answer out loud before flipping — that's the muscle the interview tests. 0 / 0 known Shuffle Reset CI vs CD vs continuous deployment? CI = auto build + test every change. Continuous delivery = every green build is release-ready, a human approves. Continuou","keywords":["the","deck","docker","run","terraform","plan","kubectl","rollout","undo"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html","page":"Glossary","section":"DevOps glossary, A–Z.","tool":"home","text":"DevOps glossary, A–Z. One crisp line per term — the vocabulary an interviewer expects you to use precisely. AWS-first, June 2026. For deep telemetry terms, pointers lead to the companion Observability guide.","keywords":["devops","glossary"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#a-f","page":"Glossary","section":"A – F","tool":"home","text":"01 A – F Artifact The immutable build output (a container image or package), versioned and promoted unchanged across environments. Auto Scaling Group (ASG) AWS construct that keeps a target number of healthy EC2 instances and scales them in/out across AZs. Blue-green Deploy strategy running two full","keywords":["terraform","plan"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#g-n","page":"Glossary","section":"G – N","tool":"home","text":"02 G – N GitOps Operating model where Git is the source of truth and an in-cluster agent (Argo CD, Flux) pulls and reconciles the cluster to it. Graviton AWS's ARM-based CPUs offering better price/performance than comparable x86 instances. HCL HashiCorp Configuration Language — the declarative synta","keywords":[]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#o-z","page":"Glossary","section":"O – Z","tool":"home","text":"03 O – Z OCI Open Container Initiative — the standard image and runtime format, so images run across any compliant tool/registry. OIDC OpenID Connect — federated identity that lets CI assume an IAM role with short-lived tokens instead of stored keys. OpenTofu The MPL-licensed, Linux Foundation fork ","keywords":["main"]}];
